/*
Time complexity

O(log n) per transformation (number of digits)

Total steps are bounded (values quickly fall below 1000)

So overall: O(log n)

Space complexity

O(log n)
Because the set stores only a small, bounded number of intermediate values

Simple intuition

Replace the number with sum of squares of its digits

If it ever becomes 1 → happy number

Otherwise, the process must eventually loop

If we see the same number again, we’re stuck in a cycle → not happy

The set exists only to catch that loop

That’s the whole idea.
*/
class Solution {
    public boolean isHappy(int n) {
        int num = n;
        
        Set<Integer> s = new HashSet<>();
        while(true){
            int sum = 0;
            while(num!=0){
            int dig = (num%10);
            sum+= (dig*dig);
            num/=10;
            }  
            if(sum==1)break;
            if(s.contains(sum)){
                return false;
            }
            else{
                s.add(sum);
            }
            
            num = sum;
        }
        
        return true;
    }
}
